From d62fa586d2dfe9bee243e3a7f57c9721dc0d199a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Agent <claude-agent@fusionpos.local>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 23:58:28 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] moe-zc: hang guard and watchdog; fix a start-up race of the
 CPU helper threads

Hang (DECISIONS D11a): the workers are pinned to one logical processor each and spin in a barrier at THREAD_PRIORITY_HIGHEST. The main thread, spinning in the CUDA driver while it waits for the GPU, was seen at dynamic priority 21; on a worker's processor it starved that worker forever (the others wait in the barrier, the GPU for the CPU, the main thread for the GPU). A watchdog thread checks the mailboxes once per second: a request waiting more than 1 s switches all workers to soft affinity (any logical processor of the P-cores, own core as ideal processor) once and logs 'hang guard'. Pinning stays hard by default (soft from the start: -2.4% on Qwen3.8); LLAMA_MOE_ZC_SOFT_PIN=1 makes it soft from the start.

After LLAMA_MOE_ZC_WATCHDOG_S seconds (default 30, 0 = off, also disables the guard) the watchdog prints the state of the mailboxes, the leader, every worker (job generation, phase / barrier; one cache line per worker), the uploader and the main thread to stderr. LLAMA_MOE_ZC_TEST_STALL_S=N makes helper 1 sleep N s before CPU job 200 (test).

Start-up race: a helper took job_gen as read when it started as its baseline; a helper that started after the first job was issued waited for the next generation and the leader for it in the barrier forever. The workers exist before any request, so the baseline is 0.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
